Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  CorrectedType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  17 REMOVED_MONTH 03 ACCESSIONED ON  18 REMOVED_MONTH 03 at 09 17 CLINICAL DATA  History of invasive ductal carcinoma in  REMOVED_CASE_ID   grade III III  with dermal and epidermal involvement                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   SIMPLE MASTECTOMY  ANGIOSARCOMA  HIGH GRADE  GRADE REMOVED_DATE OF 3   EXTENDING THROUGHOUT THE ENTIRE SPECIMEN  17 CM   AND BROADLY PRESENT AT THE POSTERIOR AND SKIN MARGINS  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE REMOVED_DATE OF 3  PRESENT WITHIN AN AREA OF DENSE SCLEROSIS CONSISTENT WITH SITE OF TREATED TUMOR  AND EXTENDING TO THE POSTERIOR MARGIN  ONE LYMPH NODE WITH NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY  SEE NOTE  NOTE  The angiosarcoma may have arisen from the invasive ductal carcinoma and thus represent a component of metaplastic carcinoma  or it may possibly be a postirradiation sarcoma  Although the angiosarcoma and invasive ductal carcinoma are juxtaposed in several areas  clear merging of the two tumor types is not identified  The angiosarcoma diffusely involves the skin and focally invades the deep skeletal muscle  The angiosarcoma tumor cells are diffusely positive for factor VIII   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and vimentin  and are focally positive for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   They are negative for cytokeratins  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  3 Cam5 2  wide spectrum  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   as well as S 100  desmin  and ulex  These immunohistochemical findings support the diagnosis of angiosarcoma  Slides of the patient s prior biopsy   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   are not currently available for comparison  Receptor studies will be reported in an addendum  Diagnosis by  MELINDA J  FAN  MD Signed On  26 REMOVED_MONTH 03 at 11 52                                                                                     IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Adden